title: Keeping the target directory small
tags: Development
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: cargo never collects target/ — 15 GB in a day — so debug info is cut down, feature builds are scoped to one crate, a script prunes superseded artifacts, and the compiler is pinned.

Cargo never garbage-collects `target/`: every dependency bump, feature change or
compiler update writes new hash-named artifacts ''next to'' the old ones
(rust-lang/cargo#13136). On this project it passed 15 GB in a day. Four measures
brought it to about 4 GB, in order of effect:

# ''Less debug info'' (about −70 %). `[profile.dev] debug = "line-tables-only"` and `[profile.dev.package."*"] debug = false` in the workspace `Cargo.toml`: each of the fifty-odd test binaries carried the full DWARF of every dependency. Backtraces keep their line numbers.
# ''Feature builds scoped to a crate.'' `cargo build --features X` on the workspace recompiles every crate against the variant — a second complete set. `cargo build -p <crate> --features X` builds the one binary that needs it.
# ''`scripts/prune-target.sh`''. A state file remembers the artifact hashes seen per crate name; when a name gains a new hash, its previous ones go. A second pass removes the artifacts of dependency versions `Cargo.lock` no longer holds, and two more trim the incremental caches. Deleting in `target/` can only cost a recompile, never corrupt anything.
# ''One pinned compiler'' (`rust-toolchain.toml`). The compiler version is part of every artifact hash: a container and its host sharing one checkout with two versions kept two complete sets and overwrote each other's binaries at every switch — 21 GB two hours after a clean. To diagnose: the `rustc` field of `target/debug/.fingerprint/*/*.json` holding several values.

''Prune only after every set has been rebuilt'' — the workspace, the test binaries,
the feature variants. Pruning between two builds of different feature sets makes
each look new and evict the other, and they are then rebuilt at every run. That is
why `scripts/complete-build.sh` builds all three before pruning, and why
`scripts/check.sh` never prunes: clippy and the tests are not every set. It warns
on a nearly full disk instead — a `target/` left alone reached 38 GB and a test died
on `No space left on device`.

What does not help, checked: `resolver.feature-unification = "workspace"` is still
nightly-only (cargo#14774, ignored with a warning on stable); the garbage collection
stabilised in 1.88 covers the global `~/.cargo` cache only; `cargo clean gc` is
nightly-only too. Thirteen copies of one library are not thirteen versions: distinct
versions × host and target × the feature sets of each invocation.

The script is generic — no metafolder assumption — and tested by
`scripts/test-prune-target.sh` (it must never delete a live artifact).
